{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{x})({e}^{2x})^{′}-({e}^{x})^{′}({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{x})(2{e}^{2x})-({e}^{x})({e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3x}-{e}^{3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}}{{e}^{x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ (-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{x}){e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ -{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{5x} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =\\frac{{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}}{{e}^{2x}} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & ={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{3x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{3}{e}^{3x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337211" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "afe4b3521ac9b8647f748f028499cb52dbe7956058ad919c2e467dbcc955fb93"} {"question" "q-337211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{x}^{3}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{x}^{3}{e}^{5x}"] ["math" "{x}^{4}{e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{x}^{5}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{x}^{5}{e}^{3x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337211" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{x}^{3}{e}^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{3}{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.$ \n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={{field-1}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${u}_{1}={u}_{1}(x),{u}_{2}={u}_{2}(x),$ that satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following constraint equation relating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Given a second-order linear inhomogeneous differential equation, the variation of parameters method requires that we seek a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}(x)+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}(x)\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the constraint\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}.\n$$\n\nHere, ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are linearly independent solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation, and ${u}_{1}$ and ${u}_{2}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nIn this case, we're told that the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our expression for ${y}_{p},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the fundamental solutions into our constraint equation for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}.\n$$\n\nWe can form a constraint that relates ${u}_{1}^{′}$ and ${u}_{2}^{′}$ by differentiating ${y}_{p}$ and equating this expression to our constraint on ${y}_{p}^{′}.$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{p}$ with respect to $x$ using the product rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={({u}_{1}{e}^{x})}^{′}+{({u}_{2}{e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & ={u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we equate our two expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} & ={u}_{1}{e}^{x}+2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}} & =\\cancel{{u}_{1}{e}^{x}}+\\cancel{2{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich finally gives\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "afe4b3521ac9b8647f748f028499cb52dbe7956058ad919c2e467dbcc955fb93"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}_{1}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}({e}^{2x})^{′}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}^{′}({e}^{x})^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}({e}^{2x})^{′}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}_{1}{e}^{x}+{u}_{2}{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337218" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Ac