(5x)-{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-337944" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}_{1}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+{u}_{2}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)=0"] "category" "ma_explicit_answer" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-338355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3{y}^{′}+2y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding homogeneous equation has the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x}$ and ${y}_{2}={e}^{-2x}.$ \n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, we should seek a particular solution \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){y}_{1}+{u}_{2}(x){y}_{2}\n$$\n\nthat satisfies the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}^{′}{y}_{2}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${u}_{2}(x)=-\\int g(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ find the function $g.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-338355" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We notice that the forcing term is $f(x)={x}^{2}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={y}_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}-{y}_{1}^{′}{y}_{2} \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x})^{′}-({e}^{-x})^{′}({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =({e}^{-x})(-2{e}^{-2x})-(-{e}^{-x})({e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-3x}\\ne 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, we have the following constraints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}^{′}{({e}^{-x})}^{′}+{u}_{2}^{′}{({e}^{-2x})}^{′}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}+{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to find an integral expression for ${u}_{2}.$ We start by eliminating ${u}_{1}^{′}$ from the second equation:\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}^{′}=-\\frac{{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}}{{e}^{-x}}=-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\,{u}_{1}^{′}{e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,(-{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-x}){e}^{-x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ -\\,{u}_{2}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {u}_{2}^{′} & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {u}_{2}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ {u}_{2} & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that $g(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14072088/state.json" "file_sha256" "9e719c887a128c0a6949089ba63db9ef7521e1b3df30b73804e06fe206de6ca2"} {"question" "q-338355" "field" nil "attribute"